WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.450
[The recording: the
capo song, heard alone]

2
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.331
Gareth Evans plays his
arrangement of Steal My Girl,

3
00:00:15.331 --> 00:00:18.077
with a capo on the third fret:

4
00:00:18.245 --> 00:00:23.128
a clamp across the strings
that raises every note.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.541
Below, a tab written by software alone:

6
00:00:28.639 --> 00:00:33.812
the best automatic tool at each step,
chained, with nothing given.

7
00:00:34.119 --> 00:00:36.749
Above, the answer key.

8
00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:39.744
The same six seconds of each.

9
00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:46.090
[The answer key's tab, played back]

10
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:52.890
[Chain-1's tab, played back]

11
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.516
The notes are nearly the same:

12
00:00:55.824 --> 00:01:01.542
ninety-four in a hundred real notes are
there, at the right pitch and time.

13
00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.413
But a tab also says where to play
each note: which string, which fret.

14
00:01:07.802 --> 00:01:10.328
Here not one is in the right place.

15
00:01:10.542 --> 00:01:12.951
Zero of eight hundred and fifteen.

16
00:01:13.247 --> 00:01:17.433
Outlined in blue: the right note,
in the wrong place.

17
00:01:17.926 --> 00:01:20.358
Gold: a note it missed.

18
00:01:20.515 --> 00:01:23.342
Striped orange: a note it made up.

19
00:01:23.754 --> 00:01:28.996
Someone playing along would find
every note somewhere his hand is not.

20
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.918
One rule, reading the lowest notes,

21
00:01:32.918 --> 00:01:37.104
missed the capo,
and every fret moved with it.

22
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.741
Told the true tuning and capo,

23
00:01:40.741 --> 00:01:44.718
the same chain puts seventy-five
in a hundred in place.

24
00:01:45.373 --> 00:01:50.632
At the end of this film,
Claude Opus 5.5, Anthropic's AI model,

25
00:01:50.632 --> 00:01:52.873
given the video and a kit of tools,

26
00:01:52.873 --> 00:01:57.698
puts ninety-three in a hundred in place.

27
00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:04.159
This film follows the job step by step,
on three recordings with known answers,

28
00:02:04.211 --> 00:02:10.451
to find which tools to give an AI for this
job, and which ones make the tab right.

29
00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:16.204
Every test in this film runs
on the same three recordings.

30
00:02:16.512 --> 00:02:22.277
Each is one guitar,
one player and one fixed camera.

31
00:02:23.357 --> 00:02:29.107
[The recording: the
capo song, heard alone]

32
00:02:29.157 --> 00:02:34.265
The first is Gareth Evans' fingerstyle
arrangement of Steal My Girl:

33
00:02:34.265 --> 00:02:38.985
standard tuning,
a capo on the third fret, steel strings.

34
00:02:39.327 --> 00:02:42.323
Standard tuning means
the open strings ring

35
00:02:42.323 --> 00:02:46.457
E, A, D, G, B, E.

36
00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:52.698
He also slaps the strings with his thumb,
and many tools hear those slaps as notes.

37
00:02:54.057 --> 00:02:59.357
[The recording: Imagine, heard alone]

38
00:02:59.407 --> 00:03:05.369
The second is his arrangement of Imagine,
in standard tuning with no capo.

39
00:03:05.781 --> 00:03:10.292
The song is famous, so a
language model may know its chords.

40
00:03:11.327 --> 00:03:17.077
[The recording: the
nylon song, heard alone]

41
00:03:17.127 --> 00:03:20.180
The third is A soft collision of breath,

42
00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:24.378
a new piece by Evan Handyside
on a nylon-string guitar.

43
00:03:24.685 --> 00:03:27.106
Every string is tuned away from standard:

44
00:03:27.204 --> 00:03:32.934
D, G, C, F, A, C.

45
00:03:34.687 --> 00:03:38.803
Each recording needs an answer key:
every note,

46
00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:42.477
with its time, its string and its fret.

47
00:03:42.477 --> 00:03:46.059
Gareth Evans shows his own
tab on screen as he plays.

48
00:03:46.471 --> 00:03:49.130
Two agents read it bar by bar, separately,

49
00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:54.465
and agreed on all but ten of the
capo song's thousand and seven note heads,

50
00:03:54.563 --> 00:03:59.967
slap marks included,
and on all of Imagine.

51
00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:03.282
Each note was then timed to the recording,

52
00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:06.463
to the moment it starts in the sound.

53
00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:10.967
Here is the capo song's key, played back.

54
00:04:10.987 --> 00:04:16.387
[The capo song's answer key, played back]

55
00:04:17.837 --> 00:04:20.809
Without the slaps,
which are not pitched notes,

56
00:04:20.809 --> 00:04:24.519
the capo key has
eight hundred and fifteen notes.

57
00:04:24.745 --> 00:04:27.503
Seven hundred and sixty-one
can be heard starting.

58
00:04:27.914 --> 00:04:32.994
The other fifty-four come from the
tab alone, like soft pull-offs.

59
00:04:34.187 --> 00:04:37.867
Separate agents checked each key
against the sound and repaired it,

60
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:43.586
Imagine's three times, until a last
check found nothing left to fix.

61
00:04:44.757 --> 00:04:47.817
Evan Handyside's tab is handwritten.

62
00:04:47.985 --> 00:04:51.851
It gives the order of the notes,
but no rhythm and no bar lines,

63
00:04:51.851 --> 00:04:56.367
so every note was placed in
time from the recording itself.

64
00:04:56.367 --> 00:05:00.814
Five hundred and fifty-six of its
six hundred and twenty-eight notes

65
00:05:00.814 --> 00:05:02.677
are confirmed in the sound.

66
00:05:03.182 --> 00:05:05.870
Its beats were placed by a rule,

67
00:05:05.887 --> 00:05:12.128
and whether its home key is
D minor or F major is a judgement.

68
00:05:12.617 --> 00:05:18.017
[The nylon song's answer key, played back]

69
00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.417
Where a key itself is in doubt,

70
00:05:20.435 --> 00:05:24.829
this film lets you listen,
and does not grade the tool.

71
00:05:26.167 --> 00:05:31.560
Turning a guitar video into a tab
is a chain of smaller jobs.

72
00:05:31.972 --> 00:05:34.265
This film counts ten.

73
00:05:35.967 --> 00:05:37.471
Split the guitar out.

74
00:05:37.569 --> 00:05:39.770
Turn the sound into notes.

75
00:05:39.938 --> 00:05:43.293
Tell the melody from the bass,
and find the taps,

76
00:05:43.293 --> 00:05:46.097
the slaps and thumps of the picking hand.

77
00:05:46.404 --> 00:05:50.126
Find the beat,
the chords and the home key.

78
00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:52.808
Read the tuning and capo.

79
00:05:53.034 --> 00:05:57.301
Choose a string and fret for every note.

80
00:05:57.497 --> 00:06:01.160
Read the hands in the video,
and join them to the sound.

81
00:06:01.328 --> 00:06:03.633
Write the tab file and play it.

82
00:06:03.685 --> 00:06:05.357
And apart from the chain,

83
00:06:05.357 --> 00:06:11.354
a tenth test sends the whole job
to a hosted service in one call.

84
00:06:12.007 --> 00:06:17.440
Each step was tested on its own, often
fed the key's version of the step before,

85
00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.488
so that only its own errors count.

86
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.310
Then whole chains ran.

87
00:06:23.557 --> 00:06:28.312
Some tools were also given a hint:
the true tuning and capo.

88
00:06:28.538 --> 00:06:33.049
That shows what a step can do
when the one before it is right.

89
00:06:34.947 --> 00:06:38.134
Here is how a tool is scored.

90
00:06:38.442 --> 00:06:40.956
Its notes are laid over the key's.

91
00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:46.296
Blue it found, gold it missed,
orange it made up.

92
00:06:46.497 --> 00:06:48.308
A note is found when it starts

93
00:06:48.308 --> 00:06:52.320
within fifty milliseconds of
a key note of the same pitch.

94
00:06:52.523 --> 00:06:54.410
That start is its onset.

95
00:06:54.694 --> 00:06:57.201
Here is Transkun, a piano model,

96
00:06:57.201 --> 00:07:01.979
on the nylon song:
the key first, then the tool.

97
00:07:02.327 --> 00:07:08.327
[The answer key's notes, played back]

98
00:07:09.127 --> 00:07:15.127
[Transkun 2.0.1's notes, played back]

99
00:07:15.927 --> 00:07:20.786
The F1 score sums this up,
from zero to one.

100
00:07:21.093 --> 00:07:24.205
It rises with every real note found,

101
00:07:24.205 --> 00:07:28.066
and falls with every
note missed or made up.

102
00:07:28.317 --> 00:07:32.445
For a finished tab,
one more score matters most:

103
00:07:32.752 --> 00:07:36.386
the share of the key's notes that
land on the right string and fret,

104
00:07:36.386 --> 00:07:37.901
at the right time.

105
00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:46.280
Step one: a separator splits a recording
into stems, one per instrument.

106
00:07:46.506 --> 00:07:50.326
Each stem was judged by the notes
found in it by Basic Pitch,

107
00:07:50.326 --> 00:07:53.385
a free note finder from Spotify.

108
00:07:54.235 --> 00:07:57.910
These recordings are one guitar alone,
so there is little to remove.

109
00:07:58.008 --> 00:08:04.423
But the capo song has thumb slaps,
and Basic Pitch hears many as notes.

110
00:08:04.623 --> 00:08:09.784
HPSS splits sound by shape:
steady tones in one part,

111
00:08:09.952 --> 00:08:13.488
short hits in the other,
with no trained model.

112
00:08:13.656 --> 00:08:15.786
The short hits.

113
00:08:16.136 --> 00:08:21.136
[HPSS short hits: the percussive part]

114
00:08:21.636 --> 00:08:23.372
The steady part.

115
00:08:23.722 --> 00:08:28.722
[HPSS steady tones: the harmonic part]

116
00:08:30.222 --> 00:08:34.373
The key's notes in these bars,
then Basic Pitch on the full recording.

117
00:08:34.723 --> 00:08:40.723
[The answer key's notes, played back]

118
00:08:41.723 --> 00:08:47.723
[Basic Pitch 0.4.0's notes from the full
recording, played back]

119
00:08:48.223 --> 00:08:52.560
Blue it found, gold it missed,
orange it made up.

120
00:08:54.660 --> 00:08:58.021
Then Basic Pitch on the steady part alone.

121
00:08:58.371 --> 00:09:04.371
[Basic Pitch 0.4.0's notes from the steady
part, played back]

122
00:09:04.971 --> 00:09:08.350
In these twenty seconds
the key has eighty-two notes.

123
00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:11.983
On the full recording
Basic Pitch found eighty,

124
00:09:11.983 --> 00:09:14.730
but wrote a hundred and fifty-seven.

125
00:09:14.747 --> 00:09:20.082
On the steady part,
seventy-nine of a hundred and five.

126
00:09:22.182 --> 00:09:24.046
Over the whole capo song,

127
00:09:24.144 --> 00:09:28.899
F1 rose from zero point six nine
to zero point seven eight.

128
00:09:29.392 --> 00:09:32.579
Imagine gained two hundredths.

129
00:09:32.805 --> 00:09:36.875
The nylon song,
with no slaps, did not move.

130
00:09:38.075 --> 00:09:44.217
Two Demucs models, a guitar fine-tune
and ElevenLabs' six-stem service

131
00:09:44.217 --> 00:09:48.472
moved these scores
by about a hundredth at most.

132
00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:53.836
Unlike the post's synthesized guitar,
which Demucs put in its piano track,

133
00:09:53.877 --> 00:09:58.434
these real guitars stayed
in its guitar stem.

134
00:09:59.683 --> 00:10:02.174
Separators are built for bands,

135
00:10:02.272 --> 00:10:05.715
so they also ran on a band clip
made for the research.

136
00:10:06.022 --> 00:10:10.243
The mix, then ElevenLabs' guitar stem.

137
00:10:10.593 --> 00:10:15.593
[The band mix S4]

138
00:10:16.593 --> 00:10:21.593
[ElevenLabs' guitar stem]

139
00:10:22.093 --> 00:10:27.173
It measured cleanest, thirteen point seven
decibels of signal over distortion,

140
00:10:27.178 --> 00:10:31.109
against one point seven five
for the six-stem Demucs

141
00:10:31.114 --> 00:10:34.406
and six point one
for its guitar fine-tune.

142
00:10:34.876 --> 00:10:40.251
It lifted Basic Pitch's F1 from
zero point six to zero point seven seven.

143
00:10:40.251 --> 00:10:42.568
It costs about a dollar forty a song.

144
00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.145
HPSS is free.

145
00:10:47.045 --> 00:10:49.861
Whether a stem sounds cleaner
is for your ear:

146
00:10:50.029 --> 00:10:54.958
Imagine,
then a Mel-Band RoFormer guitar stem.

147
00:10:55.308 --> 00:11:00.308
[The recording: Imagine]

148
00:11:01.308 --> 00:11:06.308
[Mel-Band RoFormer's guitar stem]

149
00:11:06.808 --> 00:11:10.210
For the AI's kit: on a solo guitar,

150
00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:14.401
HPSS in front of a general note model
where the strings are slapped,

151
00:11:14.401 --> 00:11:16.253
and no trained separator.

152
00:11:16.479 --> 00:11:20.108
For a band, ElevenLabs' stems.

153
00:11:22.067 --> 00:11:25.742
Step two: turn the sound into notes.

154
00:11:26.154 --> 00:11:30.258
Five tools ran on all three whole songs.

155
00:11:30.847 --> 00:11:35.497
On the capo song,
Basic Pitch found nearly every note,

156
00:11:35.503 --> 00:11:38.551
seven hundred and eighty-two
of eight hundred and fifteen.

157
00:11:38.707 --> 00:11:41.871
But it made up six hundred
and seventy-four more.

158
00:11:41.969 --> 00:11:46.933
Blue it found, gold it missed,
orange it made up.

159
00:11:47.667 --> 00:11:52.480
Two models trained on guitar,
guitar-fl and guitar-gaps,

160
00:11:52.532 --> 00:11:55.545
found as many and made up about a hundred.

161
00:11:56.049 --> 00:12:00.479
Their F1 is zero point nine two.

162
00:12:00.891 --> 00:12:05.378
Transkun, a piano model, came next.

163
00:12:05.977 --> 00:12:08.798
Two hundred and forty of
Basic Pitch's made-up notes

164
00:12:08.798 --> 00:12:12.392
start within fifty milliseconds
of a slap or a thump.

165
00:12:12.897 --> 00:12:16.380
Whether they sound like the slaps
they sit on is for you to judge:

166
00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:20.902
the key, then Basic Pitch,
then the recording.

167
00:12:21.547 --> 00:12:27.897
[The answer key's notes, played back]

168
00:12:28.447 --> 00:12:34.797
[Basic Pitch 0.4.0's notes, played back]

169
00:12:35.347 --> 00:12:41.697
[The recording]

170
00:12:42.897 --> 00:12:45.980
Given a pitch range from
the true tuning and capo,

171
00:12:45.997 --> 00:12:51.785
Basic Pitch made up fewer, six hundred
and ten, and found the same notes.

172
00:12:52.287 --> 00:12:57.320
The nylon song was harder for every tool,
worst on its low strings.

173
00:12:57.546 --> 00:13:01.349
Guitar-gaps did best,
at zero point eight three.

174
00:13:01.656 --> 00:13:05.064
In one twenty-second stretch
it missed twenty-one notes,

175
00:13:05.081 --> 00:13:08.420
one of them a note the
key's own checkers doubted.

176
00:13:09.147 --> 00:13:15.497
[The answer key's notes, played back]

177
00:13:16.047 --> 00:13:22.397
[guitar-gaps.pth's notes, played back]

178
00:13:23.697 --> 00:13:27.540
MiMo Flash,
a multimodal language model from Xiaomi,

179
00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:32.028
was sent thirty seconds of each
song and asked for the notes.

180
00:13:32.416 --> 00:13:35.836
On Imagine, in one twenty-second stretch,

181
00:13:35.841 --> 00:13:40.618
it found three of the eighty and
made up a hundred and sixteen.

182
00:13:41.427 --> 00:13:47.777
[The answer key's notes, played back]

183
00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:54.677
[MiMo v2.6 Flash's notes, played back]

184
00:13:55.327 --> 00:13:57.103
Told the tuning and capo,

185
00:13:57.103 --> 00:14:03.100
it found one note, one note
and none on the three songs.

186
00:14:03.787 --> 00:14:08.385
Basic Pitch takes about fifteen
seconds a song on two processor cores,

187
00:14:08.385 --> 00:14:11.827
the guitar models about two minutes,
with free weights.

188
00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:16.529
MiMo cost a fraction of a cent a request.

189
00:14:16.917 --> 00:14:18.827
For the AI's kit:

190
00:14:18.879 --> 00:14:24.028
a guitar-trained note model,
guitar-fl or guitar-gaps.

191
00:14:24.255 --> 00:14:27.244
Not a language model asked for the notes.

192
00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:33.373
Step three: fingerstyle guitar plays
a melody and a bass line at once,

193
00:14:33.373 --> 00:14:37.524
and on two of these songs,
taps that keep the beat.

194
00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:43.139
The simplest split puts every note
above one pitch in the melody.

195
00:14:43.434 --> 00:14:48.554
Drawn at G3, of the forty-two melody
and bass notes in these twenty seconds,

196
00:14:48.554 --> 00:14:51.231
it labels thirty-five right.

197
00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:55.503
The first six seconds
of the key's bass line,

198
00:14:55.555 --> 00:14:58.161
then the split's version.

199
00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:05.100
[The answer key's bass notes, played back]

200
00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:11.700
[The split's bass notes (split at G3),
played back]

201
00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:16.175
The seven rings are melody
notes that fell into the bass.

202
00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:21.376
Drawn at the real open third string,
F3, it gets all forty-two.

203
00:15:21.475 --> 00:15:24.929
Partitura,
a voice finder for written music,

204
00:15:25.027 --> 00:15:28.772
found seven or eight voices
where there are two.

205
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:31.260
Then the taps.

206
00:15:31.347 --> 00:15:35.817
Onsets of the HPSS short-hit
part caught all thirty-two here,

207
00:15:35.817 --> 00:15:41.001
but marked eighty-nine moments,
because every pluck starts with a hit too.

208
00:15:41.598 --> 00:15:47.154
The key's taps,
then those onsets, as clicks.

209
00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:54.000
[The answer key's taps as clicks, over the
recording]

210
00:15:54.600 --> 00:16:00.600
[HPSS onsets as clicks, over the
recording]

211
00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:07.360
Onsets of Demucs' drum stem were
precise on the capo song's slaps,

212
00:16:07.459 --> 00:16:12.074
but of Imagine's soft thumps they
caught four of twenty-nine here.

213
00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:17.600
Here are Imagine's thumps,
marked by the key's clicks.

214
00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:23.500
[Imagine: the answer key's taps as clicks,
over the recording]

215
00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:28.010
For the AI's kit, this step can wait:

216
00:16:28.178 --> 00:16:32.816
the tab it is asked for later
has no voices and no taps.

217
00:16:33.617 --> 00:16:39.486
Step four: the beat, the bars,
the chords and the home key.

218
00:16:41.167 --> 00:16:45.353
A beat tracker marks every beat,
and the first beat of each bar.

219
00:16:45.416 --> 00:16:47.210
It is scored like F1,

220
00:16:47.239 --> 00:16:51.390
with a beat found if it falls within
seventy milliseconds of the key's.

221
00:16:51.813 --> 00:16:56.742
The key's beats first,
as clicks, the bar lines higher.

222
00:16:57.087 --> 00:17:03.087
[The answer key's beats as clicks]

223
00:17:03.687 --> 00:17:08.192
Beat This! scored zero point
nine nine eight on the capo song

224
00:17:08.192 --> 00:17:11.158
and zero point nine eight nine on Imagine.

225
00:17:11.384 --> 00:17:15.570
Madmom started a bar every
two beats on the capo song:

226
00:17:15.587 --> 00:17:19.239
thirteen bar lines here,
for the key's seven.

227
00:17:19.639 --> 00:17:25.903
Madmom's, then madmom's told the tempo
and four beats to a bar.

228
00:17:27.157 --> 00:17:33.157
[madmom's beats as clicks]

229
00:17:33.757 --> 00:17:39.757
[madmom's beats as clicks, told the tempo]

230
00:17:41.157 --> 00:17:44.298
With that hint it drew all seven.

231
00:17:45.081 --> 00:17:50.050
On the nylon song, madmom agrees with
the key on tempo and bar length,

232
00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:55.293
but every tracker marks each beat
about a fifth of a second later.

233
00:17:55.547 --> 00:17:59.779
These beats were placed by a rule,
so which is right is a judgement.

234
00:17:59.947 --> 00:18:04.249
The key's beats, then Beat This!,
over the recording.

235
00:18:04.597 --> 00:18:08.597
[The answer key's beats as clicks]

236
00:18:08.597 --> 00:18:12.597
[The answer key's beats as clicks]

237
00:18:13.197 --> 00:18:17.197
[Beat This!'s beats as clicks]

238
00:18:17.197 --> 00:18:21.197
[Beat This!'s beats as clicks]

239
00:18:21.907 --> 00:18:26.098
Moved a fifth of a second earlier,
the trackers' beats score between

240
00:18:26.098 --> 00:18:31.642
zero point seven seven and zero
point eight six against the key.

241
00:18:33.737 --> 00:18:36.158
Chord names were harder.

242
00:18:36.663 --> 00:18:40.622
The best recogniser named the
right major or minor chord

243
00:18:40.622 --> 00:18:43.693
for sixty-eight to eighty-six
percent of each song:

244
00:18:44.105 --> 00:18:47.878
madmom on the capo and nylon songs,

245
00:18:47.878 --> 00:18:51.913
lv-chordia on Imagine.

246
00:18:53.167 --> 00:18:57.881
The post's strummed test clip scored
seventy-eight to ninety-seven percent,

247
00:18:57.881 --> 00:18:59.564
but the clips differ,

248
00:18:59.564 --> 00:19:04.563
so this test cannot say whether
fingerstyle chords are harder to name.

249
00:19:04.807 --> 00:19:09.614
Chordino adds colour,
like E-flat major seven,

250
00:19:09.614 --> 00:19:13.254
where the answer key names plain chords.

251
00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:15.042
That is for your ear:

252
00:19:15.268 --> 00:19:20.057
the answer key's chords, then Chordino's.

253
00:19:20.407 --> 00:19:26.407
[The answer key's chords,
played as block chords]

254
00:19:27.007 --> 00:19:33.007
[Chordino's chords, played the same way]

255
00:19:34.529 --> 00:19:37.826
Madmom named the home keys of
the capo song and Imagine,

256
00:19:37.826 --> 00:19:41.239
and said F major for the nylon song,

257
00:19:41.239 --> 00:19:44.519
whose D minor is itself a judgement.

258
00:19:44.931 --> 00:19:49.686
MiMo, asked twice,
gave two different sets of chords.

259
00:19:50.037 --> 00:19:53.961
For the AI's kit: Beat This! for the beat,

260
00:19:53.961 --> 00:19:59.343
and madmom or lv-chordia for chord names,
checked by ear.

261
00:20:02.767 --> 00:20:05.989
Step five: the tuning and the capo.

262
00:20:06.157 --> 00:20:10.819
Get them wrong and every fret in the
tab moves, as the opening showed.

263
00:20:12.417 --> 00:20:17.417
[The recording: the capo song]

264
00:20:18.017 --> 00:20:20.601
First, how far the guitar
sits from concert pitch,

265
00:20:20.629 --> 00:20:24.188
in cents, hundredths of a semitone.

266
00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:28.960
librosa came within one cent
on the capo and nylon songs,

267
00:20:29.023 --> 00:20:31.844
and three cents under on Imagine,

268
00:20:31.844 --> 00:20:35.543
whose key is itself unsure by about two.

269
00:20:37.497 --> 00:20:39.012
Then the open strings.

270
00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.909
The lowest-note rule
takes the lowest notes

271
00:20:41.909 --> 00:20:44.156
that keep coming back as the open strings.

272
00:20:44.788 --> 00:20:49.571
On the nylon song it called Drop D:
five strings wrong.

273
00:20:49.571 --> 00:20:53.119
The key's open strings, then the rule's.

274
00:20:53.467 --> 00:20:58.467
[The answer key's open
strings: D G C F A C]

275
00:20:59.067 --> 00:21:04.067
[The lowest-note rule's
open strings: Drop D]

276
00:21:04.667 --> 00:21:08.104
On the capo song,
Basic Pitch's made-up notes from

277
00:21:08.104 --> 00:21:12.429
the slaps dragged it below
what the guitar can play.

278
00:21:12.655 --> 00:21:18.803
It read the sixth string a semitone low,
and missed the capo.

279
00:21:20.407 --> 00:21:25.092
MiMo was asked three times, with the
same audio, whether there was a capo.

280
00:21:25.109 --> 00:21:29.306
It said capo one,
cannot tell, and capo one.

281
00:21:29.474 --> 00:21:34.357
Shown thirty frames, it said fret two.

282
00:21:37.357 --> 00:21:42.007
OWL v2, a detector that
finds things named in words,

283
00:21:42.024 --> 00:21:45.270
boxed the real capo on the capo song.

284
00:21:45.681 --> 00:21:48.584
On the nylon guitar, which has no capo,

285
00:21:48.584 --> 00:21:52.050
it boxed one in all six frames.

286
00:21:54.047 --> 00:21:58.268
Opus 5.5 was shown two contact sheets,

287
00:21:58.389 --> 00:22:03.213
grids of small video frames,
and three close crops of the neck.

288
00:22:03.648 --> 00:22:07.277
On the capo song the nut is out of frame,

289
00:22:07.340 --> 00:22:10.922
so it counted frets from the inlay dots.

290
00:22:11.177 --> 00:22:15.821
In its words: counting from the
twelfth-fret cluster toward the headstock,

291
00:22:15.821 --> 00:22:19.008
the capo sits in the third fret space.

292
00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:22.956
It was right about the
capo on all three songs.

293
00:22:23.182 --> 00:22:27.449
Each look cost about half a dollar,
in about a minute.

294
00:22:29.147 --> 00:22:32.416
But it called the nylon
guitar standard tuning.

295
00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:35.481
All six strings wrong.

296
00:22:35.892 --> 00:22:41.611
The key's open strings,
then the tuning Opus named.

297
00:22:41.957 --> 00:22:46.957
[The answer key's open strings again]

298
00:22:47.557 --> 00:22:52.557
[Opus 5.5's answer: standard tuning]

299
00:22:54.107 --> 00:22:57.701
No technique named the
nylon song's tuning,

300
00:22:57.927 --> 00:23:01.137
and only the picture
gave the capo reliably.

301
00:23:01.549 --> 00:23:05.932
For the AI's kit:
its own eyes for the capo.

302
00:23:06.158 --> 00:23:10.425
For an unusual tuning,
nothing here was enough.

303
00:23:13.833 --> 00:23:18.170
Step six: choose a string
and a fret for every note.

304
00:23:18.477 --> 00:23:21.263
Most pitches can be played
in several places on the neck,

305
00:23:21.263 --> 00:23:24.253
and a tab must pick one.

306
00:23:24.593 --> 00:23:27.554
Four choosers were handed
the key's own notes,

307
00:23:27.554 --> 00:23:31.530
without their strings,
plus the true tuning and capo.

308
00:23:32.023 --> 00:23:34.502
tuttut and LilyPond chose alike,

309
00:23:34.589 --> 00:23:39.472
matching the key on eighty
in a hundred notes on the capo song,

310
00:23:39.686 --> 00:23:44.812
eighty-nine on Imagine
and ninety-seven on the nylon song.

311
00:23:45.123 --> 00:23:47.956
scorify placed no notes on a whole song,

312
00:23:47.956 --> 00:23:52.571
only on ten-second pieces,
and tabsynth cannot be told a tuning.

313
00:23:53.076 --> 00:23:56.344
Guitab, which the post's
survey could not run,

314
00:23:56.443 --> 00:24:02.068
ran here in its browser page and listened
for itself, told only the capo.

315
00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:07.490
Its tab keeps its own timing,
so it is matched in note order:

316
00:24:07.658 --> 00:24:10.671
seventy-eight in a hundred
on the capo song.

317
00:24:10.978 --> 00:24:17.091
By time, only sixty-nine
of eight hundred and fifteen line up.

318
00:24:18.293 --> 00:24:22.154
Here is tuttut on the nylon
song without the tuning:

319
00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:25.857
not one of these fifty-six
notes in the key's place.

320
00:24:26.269 --> 00:24:28.922
Fifty-two keep their pitch somewhere else.

321
00:24:29.334 --> 00:24:32.631
Four low Ds come out an octave higher,

322
00:24:32.631 --> 00:24:36.167
because standard tuning
has no string that low.

323
00:24:36.799 --> 00:24:40.803
Listen for the low D:
the key, then tuttut.

324
00:24:40.803 --> 00:24:46.803
[The answer key's tab, played back]

325
00:24:47.703 --> 00:24:53.703
[tuttut 0.0.7's tab, not told the tuning]

326
00:24:54.503 --> 00:24:57.388
The rest only the eye can catch.

327
00:24:57.696 --> 00:25:00.947
At sixty seconds the key's
open F string becomes

328
00:25:00.947 --> 00:25:04.772
the fifth string at the eighth
fret: the right note,

329
00:25:04.871 --> 00:25:07.738
but a player following
this tab would reach up

330
00:25:07.738 --> 00:25:11.233
the neck for a note
the guitarist plays open.

331
00:25:11.233 --> 00:25:16.208
Given the tuning, tuttut puts
forty-nine of the fifty-six in place.

332
00:25:16.434 --> 00:25:18.977
Without the capo, the best choosers fell

333
00:25:18.977 --> 00:25:23.325
to thirty-nine in a hundred
on the capo song.

334
00:25:24.243 --> 00:25:27.273
TabCNN, which also listens for itself,

335
00:25:27.273 --> 00:25:29.665
wrote
two thousand eight hundred and sixty-four

336
00:25:29.665 --> 00:25:33.688
notes for the capo song's
eight hundred and fifteen.

337
00:25:34.297 --> 00:25:38.065
On Imagine, in these fifteen seconds,

338
00:25:38.151 --> 00:25:40.584
thirty-three are in the key's place,

339
00:25:40.601 --> 00:25:44.032
and a hundred and five match no key note.

340
00:25:44.049 --> 00:25:45.413
You can hear them.

341
00:25:45.413 --> 00:25:51.413
[The answer key's tab, played back]

342
00:25:52.313 --> 00:25:58.313
[TabCNN's tab, played back]

343
00:26:01.013 --> 00:26:07.219
For the AI's kit: tuttut or LilyPond,
given the tuning and capo.

344
00:26:07.631 --> 00:26:13.523
tuttut takes under half a second a song,
LilyPond about three and a half.

345
00:26:15.183 --> 00:26:21.470
Step seven: read the hands and
the fretboard from the video.

346
00:26:22.633 --> 00:26:28.033
[The recording: the capo song]

347
00:26:28.133 --> 00:26:31.895
MediaPipe, Google's hand tracker,
followed the fretting hand

348
00:26:31.895 --> 00:26:36.057
in four hundred and ninety-five
of these five hundred frames.

349
00:26:36.562 --> 00:26:43.290
Over the three songs it lost that hand
in one to twelve frames in a hundred.

350
00:26:44.583 --> 00:26:47.706
There is no answer key for hand points,

351
00:26:47.706 --> 00:26:51.996
so whether the skeleton follows
the hand is for your eye.

352
00:26:52.501 --> 00:26:54.945
RTMPose, a second tracker,

353
00:26:55.102 --> 00:26:59.972
lost the hand in a fifth of
the thirty seconds from here.

354
00:27:00.733 --> 00:27:03.786
To turn a fingertip into
a string and a fret,

355
00:27:03.786 --> 00:27:08.924
a fret grid was fitted to an
outline of the neck set by hand.

356
00:27:09.336 --> 00:27:14.358
Around the capo,
it numbers every fret one too high.

357
00:27:14.683 --> 00:27:18.497
Each fretted note's fingertip cell
was then checked against the key.

358
00:27:18.897 --> 00:27:21.539
On the capo song it was exactly right

359
00:27:21.637 --> 00:27:25.190
for two of fifty notes in
a twenty-second stretch,

360
00:27:25.190 --> 00:27:29.329
and for nine in a hundred over the song.

361
00:27:30.983 --> 00:27:36.492
On Imagine, for none of sixty-seven
notes in twenty seconds,

362
00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:40.057
and three in a hundred over the song.

363
00:27:40.561 --> 00:27:45.954
The grid held the neck in only
four of those twenty seconds.

364
00:27:47.883 --> 00:27:53.283
[The recording: the nylon song]

365
00:27:53.383 --> 00:27:57.998
On the nylon guitar,
no detector found a single fret line,

366
00:27:58.004 --> 00:28:00.622
so there are no cells at all.

367
00:28:00.918 --> 00:28:04.256
CoTracker three,
run without a graphics card,

368
00:28:04.273 --> 00:28:09.608
drifted ninety-five pixels off
the neck in twenty seconds.

369
00:28:09.783 --> 00:28:13.504
MediaPipe takes three
to five minutes a song.

370
00:28:13.916 --> 00:28:17.376
For the AI's kit:
a hand tracker finds the hand,

371
00:28:17.376 --> 00:28:20.853
but no tool here read the
string and fret from it.

372
00:28:22.350 --> 00:28:27.476
Step eight:
join what is heard to what is seen.

373
00:28:29.100 --> 00:28:34.800
[The recording: the capo song]

374
00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:37.264
Every note's onset was
looked up in the video

375
00:28:37.264 --> 00:28:39.964
at that moment, against the fingertips.

376
00:28:40.468 --> 00:28:43.557
Of sixty-four fretted
notes in twenty seconds,

377
00:28:43.557 --> 00:28:48.764
the cell under a fingertip was
exactly the key's for twelve.

378
00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:55.841
Those cells can pin a note to a string
before LilyPond chooses the rest.

379
00:28:56.473 --> 00:28:59.236
Without the capo, that helped:

380
00:28:59.236 --> 00:29:03.335
in these twenty seconds sixty notes moved
into the key's place,

381
00:29:03.335 --> 00:29:05.604
and twenty-one moved out.

382
00:29:06.109 --> 00:29:11.804
Over the song,
thirty-nine in a hundred became sixty-one.

383
00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:18.950
The pins change places, not pitches,
so both tabs sound like this.

384
00:29:19.455 --> 00:29:22.074
Watch the moved notes light.

385
00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:28.420
[The answer key's tab, played back: the
pinned tab sounds the same]

386
00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:34.025
With the true tuning and capo,
the pins only got in the way:

387
00:29:34.124 --> 00:29:38.303
all twelve pinned runs scored
lower than the chooser alone,

388
00:29:38.303 --> 00:29:40.852
by five to thirty in a hundred,

389
00:29:40.950 --> 00:29:46.924
the nylon song's on a grid that
borrowed the key maker's fret count.

390
00:29:48.120 --> 00:29:53.583
Once the tuning and capo were right,
seeing the fingers made the strings worse:

391
00:29:53.809 --> 00:29:57.275
the cells were rarely the played position.

392
00:29:57.687 --> 00:30:03.370
For the AI's kit: leave the pins out
once the tuning and capo are known.

393
00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:09.638
Step nine: write the tab as
a file a guitarist can open,

394
00:30:09.638 --> 00:30:11.699
draw it, and play it back.

395
00:30:12.111 --> 00:30:16.703
The format here is Guitar Pro 5.

396
00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:21.614
The research's first writer
gave the whole song one tempo.

397
00:30:21.922 --> 00:30:26.386
Fed the key's own tab,
it kept nearly every note, in order.

398
00:30:26.693 --> 00:30:29.033
But a player speeds up and slows down.

399
00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:33.468
The recording on the left,
the one-tempo file on the right.

400
00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:37.720
[The one-tempo file over the recording]

401
00:30:37.720 --> 00:30:41.720
[The one-tempo file over the recording]

402
00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:44.397
The notes drift off their times:

403
00:30:44.565 --> 00:30:47.886
only forty-four in a hundred
of the capo song's notes

404
00:30:47.886 --> 00:30:50.365
landed within fifty milliseconds,

405
00:30:50.405 --> 00:30:54.092
and seven in a hundred on the nylon song.

406
00:30:54.712 --> 00:30:58.880
A writer that sets a new
tempo on every beat,

407
00:30:58.880 --> 00:31:00.970
on a grid of thirty-second notes,

408
00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:05.342
kept ninety-four to a
hundred in a hundred.

409
00:31:05.753 --> 00:31:09.312
The same bars from that file.

410
00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:14.560
[The per-beat file over the recording]

411
00:31:14.560 --> 00:31:18.560
[The per-beat file over the recording]

412
00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:21.899
As in the post, no note was lost:

413
00:31:22.067 --> 00:31:25.997
what one tempo loses is the timing.

414
00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:30.185
alphaTab draws the file in a browser.

415
00:31:30.352 --> 00:31:35.119
With a tempo change on most beats,
it prints a tempo mark over most of them.

416
00:31:35.345 --> 00:31:38.288
Its first bars sound like this.

417
00:31:38.540 --> 00:31:43.540
[The drawn file's first bars, played back]

418
00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:50.619
Its metronome keeps to a one-tempo
file within three milliseconds.

419
00:31:51.031 --> 00:31:52.958
With a tempo on most beats,

420
00:31:52.958 --> 00:31:57.678
its clicks fell up to a hundred
and thirteen milliseconds off.

421
00:31:58.182 --> 00:32:00.871
Here is its metronome over the recording:

422
00:32:01.097 --> 00:32:05.259
the recording on the left,
its clicks on the right.

423
00:32:05.510 --> 00:32:11.510
[alphaTab's metronome
right, the recording left]

424
00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:15.330
So every tab in this film
is played by FluidSynth,

425
00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:20.097
a software synthesizer, from the file's
notes, on the recording's clock.

426
00:32:20.323 --> 00:32:24.491
For the AI's kit: a tempo on every beat,

427
00:32:24.491 --> 00:32:28.328
and FluidSynth to check the tab by ear.

428
00:32:29.700 --> 00:32:32.667
The tenth test skipped the chain.

429
00:32:33.079 --> 00:32:35.865
Hosted services that
promise a tab in one step

430
00:32:35.865 --> 00:32:39.192
were offered the thirty-second segments.

431
00:32:40.590 --> 00:32:43.887
Klangio's Guitar to Tabs
stopped before the upload,

432
00:32:43.887 --> 00:32:49.466
at a box asking to confirm the rights to
the music, where this test always stops.

433
00:32:49.878 --> 00:32:53.820
Fretwise answered with a
check that the user is human.

434
00:32:54.324 --> 00:32:57.227
Five more needed an application,
an account,

435
00:32:57.227 --> 00:33:01.784
a paid plan, a rights box or a balance.

436
00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:08.892
MiMo Flash, asked for the whole
tab in one prompt, did answer.

437
00:33:09.164 --> 00:33:14.447
On Imagine its tab has two of the key's
hundred and twenty-five notes in place,

438
00:33:14.447 --> 00:33:18.795
and its chords name one C
for all thirty seconds.

439
00:33:18.940 --> 00:33:21.430
The key, then MiMo.

440
00:33:22.680 --> 00:33:28.680
[The answer key's tab, played back]

441
00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:35.280
[MiMo v2.6 Flash's tab, played back]

442
00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:39.235
The sound went first under a file
name that named the song's test,

443
00:33:39.235 --> 00:33:42.202
like capo, then again as plain clip.

444
00:33:42.335 --> 00:33:48.286
With the same sound and prompt, MiMo
changed its capo answer on every song.

445
00:33:48.651 --> 00:33:52.616
All fourteen calls cost under three cents.

446
00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:58.489
For the AI's kit:
none of the one-call services tested here.

447
00:34:00.500 --> 00:34:05.104
Now the whole chain,
from the recording to a finished file.

448
00:34:05.736 --> 00:34:10.967
Six chains ran on each song,
one technique per step.

449
00:34:11.640 --> 00:34:15.506
Chain one is blind:
the best tool at each step,

450
00:34:15.506 --> 00:34:19.019
with the tuning and capo
from the lowest-note rule.

451
00:34:19.256 --> 00:34:23.012
Chain two is the same,
given the true tuning and capo.

452
00:34:23.180 --> 00:34:25.479
Chain three is blind too,

453
00:34:25.479 --> 00:34:30.617
with Opus 5.5 reading the capo and
the fingertips pinning strings.

454
00:34:31.121 --> 00:34:33.652
On the nylon song its fret grid borrowed

455
00:34:33.652 --> 00:34:38.330
the key maker's fret count,
which changed nothing.

456
00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:40.414
Chain four is the one hosted call.

457
00:34:40.582 --> 00:34:43.659
Chains five and six are
each given part of the key,

458
00:34:43.659 --> 00:34:49.122
its notes or its strings,
to show what the other steps alone can do.

459
00:34:50.260 --> 00:34:56.437
Blind, the capo song scored zero and
the nylon song eight in a hundred,

460
00:34:56.437 --> 00:34:59.844
both sunk by the tuning and capo answer.

461
00:35:00.152 --> 00:35:06.718
Imagine, in standard tuning with no capo,
scored eighty-two.

462
00:35:07.270 --> 00:35:12.663
With Opus reading the capo, the capo
song rose to seventy-one in a hundred.

463
00:35:12.889 --> 00:35:15.101
Given the true tuning and capo,

464
00:35:15.118 --> 00:35:21.591
the three songs reached seventy-five,
seventy-one and eighty-two.

465
00:35:21.591 --> 00:35:26.010
[The recording plays on: the capo song]

466
00:35:26.950 --> 00:35:30.096
Even given the key's string
for every note it found,

467
00:35:30.096 --> 00:35:33.980
the nylon song's chain still
misses and makes up notes:

468
00:35:34.183 --> 00:35:37.417
here twenty-four missed
and twenty-eight made up.

469
00:35:37.805 --> 00:35:41.650
Listen for the gaps:
the key, then chain six.

470
00:35:41.700 --> 00:35:47.700
[The answer key's tab, played back]

471
00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:54.600
[Chain-6's tab, played back]

472
00:35:56.450 --> 00:35:57.954
Where does the score go?

473
00:35:58.052 --> 00:36:03.202
Step by step, a wrong tuning or capo cost
up to seventy-five notes in a hundred.

474
00:36:03.509 --> 00:36:06.945
Finding the notes
cost six to twenty-seven,

475
00:36:06.945 --> 00:36:09.425
the string choice nine to twenty-one,

476
00:36:09.523 --> 00:36:15.276
the video pins up to ten,
and writing the file at most six.

477
00:36:17.867 --> 00:36:20.393
Then the test this film builds to.

478
00:36:20.619 --> 00:36:24.508
Opus 5.5 was given one of
the three videos and asked

479
00:36:24.508 --> 00:36:29.263
to write down what the guitarist plays,
as a tab.

480
00:36:30.667 --> 00:36:34.392
[The blind video's sound: the nylon song]

481
00:36:34.392 --> 00:36:38.117
[The blind video's sound: the nylon song]

482
00:36:38.217 --> 00:36:41.369
This was the whole prompt, with the video.

483
00:36:41.537 --> 00:36:43.715
Nothing else about the piece.

484
00:36:43.715 --> 00:36:48.782
[The blind video's sound: the nylon song]

485
00:36:48.782 --> 00:36:53.850
[The blind video's sound: the nylon song]

486
00:36:53.850 --> 00:36:58.917
[The blind video's sound: the nylon song]

487
00:36:59.017 --> 00:37:03.853
Each song was done twice, each time
in a fresh session with no memory:

488
00:37:04.079 --> 00:37:06.935
once with a kit of the tools
that measured best here,

489
00:37:06.935 --> 00:37:11.103
and a sheet of their accuracy,
and once bare, with Python,

490
00:37:11.103 --> 00:37:14.766
its number libraries and the
same file writer and checker.

491
00:37:15.178 --> 00:37:19.375
The film calls these the
kit arm and the bare arm.

492
00:37:19.627 --> 00:37:24.591
With the kit, the tuning and capo are
read the way the best technique did,

493
00:37:24.619 --> 00:37:30.756
from frames and by ear, with the
lowest-note rule only as evidence.

494
00:37:31.179 --> 00:37:36.781
Same model, told to think as hard
as it can, ninety minutes each.

495
00:37:38.177 --> 00:37:41.121
The attempts built their
own tools as they went.

496
00:37:41.219 --> 00:37:44.952
On the capo song the kit
arm cut strips of the neck,

497
00:37:45.050 --> 00:37:48.121
one a second, to see where the hand was.

498
00:37:48.533 --> 00:37:52.817
On Imagine the bare arm drew
its own picture of the sound,

499
00:37:52.817 --> 00:37:55.424
and wrote its own note finder.

500
00:37:56.827 --> 00:37:59.457
Two things to know first.

501
00:37:59.474 --> 00:38:02.237
This is the second run: in the first,

502
00:38:02.237 --> 00:38:07.380
the software running the test showed
each attempt the test's own task list,

503
00:38:07.380 --> 00:38:10.481
which named the performers
and the answer keys,

504
00:38:10.481 --> 00:38:12.913
so it was run again with an empty list.

505
00:38:13.139 --> 00:38:15.670
And the kit was picked, and measured,

506
00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:19.693
on these same recordings,
which favours it.

507
00:38:20.347 --> 00:38:22.646
The attempts were told which tools to use,

508
00:38:22.646 --> 00:38:25.961
but the others were not taken away,
web search among them.

509
00:38:26.373 --> 00:38:31.882
The audits found no web search, outside
service, skill or helper agent used.

510
00:38:32.189 --> 00:38:34.895
Two attempts looked up
a tool's description,

511
00:38:34.895 --> 00:38:37.501
and one stopped its own command.

512
00:38:38.897 --> 00:38:41.806
The answer keys stayed
on the same machine.

513
00:38:41.904 --> 00:38:44.981
A file watch logged every
read of their folders,

514
00:38:44.981 --> 00:38:48.319
and an auditor read every
attempt's transcript.

515
00:38:48.731 --> 00:38:52.289
All six attempts were found valid.

516
00:38:53.887 --> 00:38:56.296
All six handed in a tab,

517
00:38:56.348 --> 00:39:00.383
a Guitar Pro file and a
note on how they worked,

518
00:39:00.481 --> 00:39:03.634
in thirty-two to forty-three minutes.

519
00:39:04.139 --> 00:39:07.233
Each said it did not recognise the piece.

520
00:39:09.567 --> 00:39:11.791
Here is what each attempt wrote,

521
00:39:11.889 --> 00:39:16.086
against the key and
against the fixed chains.

522
00:39:17.787 --> 00:39:22.787
[The recording: the capo song]

523
00:39:23.387 --> 00:39:24.821
The capo song.

524
00:39:25.326 --> 00:39:28.298
With the kit, Opus
merged both guitar models

525
00:39:28.426 --> 00:39:31.079
and read capo three from the inlays,

526
00:39:31.131 --> 00:39:34.109
overruling its own lowest-note tool.

527
00:39:34.416 --> 00:39:40.402
Bare, it wrote its own note finder,
with templates to set the slaps aside.

528
00:39:42.097 --> 00:39:45.034
In these twenty seconds the kit arm has

529
00:39:45.034 --> 00:39:48.192
eighty-three of the key's
ninety notes in place,

530
00:39:48.192 --> 00:39:51.554
the bare arm sixty-eight,
missing seventeen.

531
00:39:51.722 --> 00:39:56.604
Blue it found, gold it missed,
orange it made up.

532
00:39:56.772 --> 00:40:01.573
The key, the kit arm, then the bare arm.

533
00:40:01.927 --> 00:40:07.927
[The answer key's tab, played back]

534
00:40:08.527 --> 00:40:14.527
[The kit arm's tab, played back]

535
00:40:15.127 --> 00:40:21.127
[The bare arm's tab, played back]

536
00:40:21.827 --> 00:40:27.377
[The recording plays on: the capo song]

537
00:40:27.377 --> 00:40:28.962
Over the whole song,

538
00:40:29.060 --> 00:40:33.908
ninety-three in a hundred with the kit,
eighty-two bare.

539
00:40:34.413 --> 00:40:37.710
The kit arm beat every chain but

540
00:40:37.710 --> 00:40:41.048
the one given the key's strings.

541
00:40:43.547 --> 00:40:48.547
[The recording: Imagine]

542
00:40:49.147 --> 00:40:50.347
Imagine.

543
00:40:50.668 --> 00:40:55.481
Both arms named standard tuning,
no capo, C major.

544
00:40:56.183 --> 00:41:00.879
With the kit, Opus compared the
two guitar models note by note.

545
00:41:01.152 --> 00:41:05.605
Bare,
it wrote its own onset and pitch pickers.

546
00:41:07.307 --> 00:41:12.503
Here the kit arm has sixty-five
of seventy-four notes in place.

547
00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:18.145
The bare arm has thirty-seven,
and made up sixty more.

548
00:41:18.497 --> 00:41:24.497
[The answer key's tab, played back]

549
00:41:25.097 --> 00:41:31.097
[The kit arm's tab, played back]

550
00:41:31.697 --> 00:41:37.697
[The bare arm's tab, played back]

551
00:41:38.397 --> 00:41:43.947
[The recording plays on: Imagine]

552
00:41:43.947 --> 00:41:48.969
Over the song, eighty-six in a
hundred against seventy-one.

553
00:41:49.137 --> 00:41:53.578
More than a third of the bare arm's
notes match nothing in the key.

554
00:41:56.077 --> 00:42:01.077
[The recording: the nylon song]

555
00:42:01.677 --> 00:42:03.169
The nylon song.

556
00:42:03.349 --> 00:42:08.411
Both arms got five strings right and
tuned the first a whole step too high,

557
00:42:08.411 --> 00:42:13.827
D for C, the kit arm from the chord
shapes, against its own rule's Drop D.

558
00:42:14.076 --> 00:42:17.624
With the kit,
Opus merged both guitar models.

559
00:42:17.757 --> 00:42:22.680
Bare, it wrote its own note finder
and kept to the first four frets,

560
00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:24.915
as the video shows.

561
00:42:26.617 --> 00:42:29.874
So every note the key plays
on the first string moved.

562
00:42:30.100 --> 00:42:34.030
The ear cannot catch a note in the
wrong place, but the key shows it.

563
00:42:34.663 --> 00:42:37.595
Here the arms differ more
in the notes they found:

564
00:42:38.007 --> 00:42:42.750
forty-four in place with the kit,
twenty-four bare.

565
00:42:43.097 --> 00:42:49.097
[The answer key's tab, played back]

566
00:42:49.697 --> 00:42:55.697
[The kit arm's tab, played back]

567
00:42:56.297 --> 00:43:02.297
[The bare arm's tab, played back]

568
00:43:02.997 --> 00:43:07.697
[The recording plays on: the nylon song]

569
00:43:08.547 --> 00:43:12.176
Where the beat falls in this piece
is a judgement, so it is not scored.

570
00:43:12.402 --> 00:43:16.878
The key's beats, the kit arm's,
then the bare arm's.

571
00:43:17.227 --> 00:43:23.227
[The answer key's beats as clicks]

572
00:43:23.827 --> 00:43:29.827
[The kit arm's beats as clicks]

573
00:43:30.427 --> 00:43:36.427
[The bare arm's beats as clicks]

574
00:43:38.277 --> 00:43:40.982
Over the song, fifty-five in a hundred

575
00:43:40.982 --> 00:43:44.889
with the kit, and thirty-five bare.

576
00:43:45.197 --> 00:43:48.935
Both sit between the blind chain,
at eight,

577
00:43:48.935 --> 00:43:53.481
and the chain given the true tuning,
at seventy-one.

578
00:43:55.977 --> 00:44:00.517
Across the three songs, the kit arm
beat the bare arm on every one,

579
00:44:00.517 --> 00:44:03.483
by eleven to twenty notes in a hundred.

580
00:44:03.860 --> 00:44:08.139
Both named the capo and
tempo right on every song.

581
00:44:08.516 --> 00:44:11.413
Neither found the nylon
song's first string.

582
00:44:11.558 --> 00:44:17.311
For its home key, the kit arm said
D minor and the bare arm F major,

583
00:44:17.328 --> 00:44:19.865
which share their notes.

584
00:44:22.167 --> 00:44:24.507
Most of that gap came
from finding the notes:

585
00:44:24.675 --> 00:44:27.850
the kit arm found more, and made up fewer.

586
00:44:28.483 --> 00:44:30.242
Once a note was found,

587
00:44:30.259 --> 00:44:35.072
on the steel-string songs both arms
placed it right about equally often.

588
00:44:35.704 --> 00:44:41.504
On the nylon song, the kit arm also
placed more of its found notes right.

589
00:44:43.257 --> 00:44:46.961
An earlier test found Opus
sometimes did better without tools,

590
00:44:46.961 --> 00:44:51.588
but here the kit arm did better
on every song, in both rounds.

591
00:44:53.287 --> 00:44:58.175
Round one ran first,
with the same videos, prompt, model,

592
00:44:58.175 --> 00:45:02.662
time and kit, while each attempt
could see the task list.

593
00:45:03.074 --> 00:45:08.159
It put the arms in the same order,
and where an arm kept its tuning answer,

594
00:45:08.159 --> 00:45:12.879
its two runs differed by one
to four notes in a hundred:

595
00:45:12.977 --> 00:45:15.921
that is how much one attempt varies.

596
00:45:18.333 --> 00:45:22.698
So which tools should
an AI be given for this job,

597
00:45:22.698 --> 00:45:25.003
and which ones make the tab right?

598
00:45:25.787 --> 00:45:29.450
Measured on these three recordings,
step by step:

599
00:45:31.653 --> 00:45:35.333
For the capo, its own eyes: frames,

600
00:45:35.333 --> 00:45:38.474
contact sheets and close
crops of the neck.

601
00:45:38.886 --> 00:45:42.991
That was the only way the capo was
read right on all three songs.

602
00:45:43.298 --> 00:45:48.946
For the tuning, eyes and ears together
still missed the nylon guitar's.

603
00:45:49.593 --> 00:45:53.378
To split the guitar out,
HPSS helps a general note

604
00:45:53.378 --> 00:45:57.494
model like Basic Pitch where
the strings are slapped.

605
00:45:57.906 --> 00:46:01.000
In front of the guitar-trained
models it did not help.

606
00:46:01.633 --> 00:46:07.072
For a band, ElevenLabs' stems
measured cleanest.

607
00:46:07.423 --> 00:46:11.423
[A replay: HPSS's steady
part of the capo song]

608
00:46:12.023 --> 00:46:17.706
For notes, a guitar-trained model,
guitar-fl or guitar-gaps.

609
00:46:18.053 --> 00:46:22.053
[A replay: guitar-fl's notes, played back]

610
00:46:22.653 --> 00:46:26.107
Beat This! for the beat.

611
00:46:26.453 --> 00:46:30.453
[A replay: Beat This!'s beats as clicks]

612
00:46:31.053 --> 00:46:35.366
Madmom or lv-chordia for chord names,
checked by ear.

613
00:46:35.755 --> 00:46:41.079
tuttut or LilyPond for strings and frets,
given the tuning and capo.

614
00:46:41.386 --> 00:46:44.497
Then a file with a tempo on every beat,

615
00:46:44.497 --> 00:46:49.600
drawn with alphaTab and played with
FluidSynth against the recording.

616
00:46:49.953 --> 00:46:53.953
[A replay: the per-beat file over the
recording]

617
00:46:54.853 --> 00:47:00.403
Leave out what did not help here: video
pins once the tuning and capo are known,

618
00:47:00.403 --> 00:47:05.366
a language model asked for notes,
and the one-call services.

619
00:47:07.963 --> 00:47:13.879
All of these tools ran without a graphics
card, in seconds to minutes a song.

620
00:47:14.290 --> 00:47:19.863
The only paid ones were ElevenLabs' stems,
about a dollar forty a song,

621
00:47:19.863 --> 00:47:24.885
and each look by Opus at the frames,
about half a dollar.

622
00:47:27.183 --> 00:47:31.322
What still fails:
the nylon guitar's tuning,

623
00:47:31.339 --> 00:47:34.283
which no tool and no attempt found.

624
00:47:34.590 --> 00:47:39.798
Notes on nylon strings, where the best
model reached zero point eight three.

625
00:47:40.105 --> 00:47:42.886
And chord names from the tools alone,

626
00:47:42.984 --> 00:47:48.146
right sixty-eight to eighty-six
percent of the time.

627
00:47:49.493 --> 00:47:54.967
These are three recordings by two players,
with one run of most tools.

628
00:47:55.310 --> 00:47:58.288
No strumming, no electric guitar,

629
00:47:58.537 --> 00:48:02.142
no band, no moving camera.

630
00:48:02.392 --> 00:48:08.133
They show where these tools stand
on this job, not on every guitar.

631
00:48:10.333 --> 00:48:15.407
The last word goes to the guitar,
on the kit arm's best twenty seconds,

632
00:48:15.407 --> 00:48:19.523
where it found every note of the key,
with three extra.

633
00:48:19.830 --> 00:48:24.376
Hear the key, the kit arm's tab,
and the recording.

634
00:48:24.723 --> 00:48:30.723
[The answer key's tab, played back]

635
00:48:31.323 --> 00:48:37.990
[The kit arm's tab, played back]

636
00:48:37.990 --> 00:48:44.656
[The kit arm's tab, played back]

637
00:48:44.656 --> 00:48:51.323
[The kit arm's tab, played back]

638
00:48:53.873 --> 00:48:59.873
[The recording]

639
00:49:02.500 --> 00:49:06.122
The recordings are by
Gareth Evans and Evan Handyside.

640
00:49:06.122 --> 00:49:10.180
This film was made by
Claude agents, for aaronorelup.com.

641
00:49:10.380 --> 00:49:16.280
[The nylon song's last bars, under the
credits]

642
00:49:16.280 --> 00:49:22.180
[The nylon song's last bars, under the
credits]

643
00:49:22.180 --> 00:49:28.080
[The nylon song's last bars, under the
credits]

644
00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:33.980
[The nylon song's last bars, under the
